  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday and Sunday: The upper low offshore continues to fill in and \nslide to the south towards the northern California coastline \nSaturday evening. This will bring weak synoptic ascent into the \nregion Saturday afternoon and evening, bringing small chances for \nlight rain (5-15% chance over central WA, 10-30% chance for eastern \nWA and north ID). PWATs increase to 110-130% of normal Saturday \nafternoon and evening but dry air near the surface will only allow \nfor sprinkles to a light drizzle mainly east of Ritzville. Rain \namounts will be very light around a trace to a tenth of an inch \nthrough Saturday night. Light rain continues into Sunday for \nsoutheast WA and the central and southern ID Panhandle ahead of \ndrier air filtering in from the west. Clouds will erode in the late \nmorning and early afternoon Sunday in central and eastern WA and the \nlate afternoon around sunset in north ID. The chances of a tenth of \nan inch or more of rain from Saturday afternoon to Sunday are around \n20-50% south of I-90 and east of US-395. \n\nMonday through Saturday: High pressure builds into the region Monday \nbringing dry conditions and seasonal temperatures in the upper 40s \nto mid 50s. This ridge will be slightly disrupted Monday night into \nTuesday by a shortwave moving through central BC and AB, bringing a \nsmall chance for snow showers in the Cascades above 3500 feet. \nImpacts look very minimal from this system. The ridge strengthens \nbehind this weak wave Wednesday but chances for precipitation \nincrease Wednesday night as troughing approaches the region. Models \nare in some disagreement with the position and strength of the \nplacement of this trough but the overall message is there is a \nsignal for unsettled conditions to return mid to late next week. \nGlobal ensembles continue to show a 60/40 split towards dry \nconditions continuing but this will change in the coming days as \nconfidence increases. /DB",
